Subject: [PATCH openEuler-26.09 00/26] mm, fs, entry: improve concurrent
 fork/exec scalability

UnixBench shell, execl, and other process-heavy workloads repeatedly
create and tear down dynamically linked processes. At high concurrency,
many tasks insert and remove VMAs for the same executable, dynamic loader,
and shared libraries. Those file VMAs are serialized by one
address_space::i_mmap_rwsem and one interval tree, making the lock a
cross-CPU and cross-NUMA bottleneck.

Patches 1-19 add file-VMA batching and optional NUMA-local i_mmap shards.
Mappings begin on the existing central tree and move to per-node shards
only after write-lock contention. The series updates all write walkers,
read walkers, architecture cache walkers, and all-lock users so every
published root remains visible under the required locking contract. Dynamic
shard state is reset for every inode lifetime so slab-object reuse cannot
inherit a previous file's contention state. The shard-aware file-rmap callback
also preserves the baseline invalid-address diagnostic invariant.

CONFIG_I_MMAP_SHARDS is default n. Every source change in patches 1-19 is
compile-time isolated: with the option disabled, removing the conditional
blocks leaves the same C and header contents as the pre-series tree at
every intermediate commit. With the capability built, i_mmap_opt=on is
still required at early boot to enable batching and dynamic sharding.

Patches 20-26 carry the related RISC-V and LoongArch entry conversions,
the syscall-exit inline optimization, and the VFS permission lookup
optimizations used by the measured fork/exec workload.

The supplied UnixBench result reports the following aggregate scores:

                         baseline    mmap-opt       change
  1 container              1547.9      2055.5       +32.79%
  80 containers            4201.1      4199.0        -0.05%
  linearity                  36.8%       48.9%       +12.1 pp

Sharding trades some read-side cost for write-side scalability. A read
walk over a sharded mapping must cover every shard, so operations such as
truncate and reclaim can be slower when they target that mapping. The
cost is mapping-local and applies only after sustained write contention
has triggered sharding; unsharded and unrelated files keep the original
single-tree path.

Targeted testing confirms this trade-off when truncating a highly shared,
already-sharded file. Its practical impact on the intended workload is
limited: the mappings most likely to become sharded are executables,
loaders, and shared libraries, which are normally stable and rarely
truncated or hole-punched while mapped. Global reclaim selects folios from
the wider reclaimable set and performs file rmap one mapping at a time, so
the additional cost is paid only when a selected folio belongs to an
already-sharded mapping. Such executable and library folios normally form
only a small part of the reclaimable working set, and the extra tree walk
is only one stage of the complete reclaim process. The improvement in
fork/exec-heavy workloads therefore outweighs this uncommon read-side
case, while i_mmap_opt=off remains available for deployments that prefer
the original behavior.

